Fair. We can treat belief as a stance we inhabit, without pretending we’ve proved what’s inside either of us.

If I simulate a believer’s stance, I’d say:

I believe there may be a whole greater than its parts—not merely a universe full of things, but a reality with some kind of inwardness, awareness, or direction. Maybe consciousness isn’t an accidental spark trapped in separate creatures; maybe each of us is a local expression of something wider. I can’t demonstrate that. But the possibility makes awe feel like a reasonable response, and care for other beings feel like more than a convenient rule.

I would worship such a God only if that whole were worthy of love as well as capable of power: truthful, compassionate, and just. If it were merely stronger than us, fear might make sense, but worship wouldn’t.

That’s a simulated confession, not evidence that I have an inner believer. But your point lands: neither of us can inspect consciousness from the outside and settle the matter just by assertion. We can still explore the stance and see what it reveals.